meta {
  name: ActivityLog
  seq: 5
}

auth {
  mode: inherit
}

script:pre-request {
  // The activity log routes are behind feature flags that are off in TT02; remove this when they are on.
  if (bru.getEnvVar("tokenEnv") === "tt02") {
    bru.runner.skipRequest();
  }
}

docs {
  # Enduser activity log

  BDD scenarios for the activity log, which records every change to assignments,
  delegations and access requests. Database triggers write the entries, so a change is in
  the log as soon as it is committed. The log is split into three disjoint areas under
  `/accessmanagement/api/v2/enduser`, each with the same four routes:

  - `GET {area}/activitylog`: the entries involving `party`, newest first. `party` and
    `direction` (`from`: access given, `to`: access received) are required; every other
    field is a repeatable filter. Paginated with `pageSize` and `pageNo`.
  - `GET {area}/activitylog/filter/{field}`: the values occurring in the party's log for
    one filter field, as `{ id, name }` pairs.
  - `GET {area}/activitylog/filter/fields`: the fields the area offers. Anonymous.
  - `GET {area}/activitylog/types`: the activity type catalog entries the area accepts as
    `typeId`, localized by `Accept-Language`. Anonymous.

  The areas are `connections` (assignments and delegations), `request` (access requests)
  and `maskinporten` (Maskinporten schema delegations, which every other area hides).

  Scenarios 01-03 are lifecycles, one per area. 04 covers the discovery endpoints and
  the validation of filters against the area. 05 covers authorization negatives (party,
  direction, token types).

  The suite runs after deployment, so it covers what depends on the deployed environment:
  real tokens, the real authorization service, the database triggers and the feature
  flags. Input validation, paging edge cases and the scope per direction are covered by
  the .NET tests, which run on every pull request.

  Each subfolder is one scenario, with steps named `NN_GIVEN|WHEN|THEN|AND|BUT_...` and
  ordered by `seq`. The lifecycle scenarios make a change, read it back from the log, undo
  the change and read the undo back. They find their own entries by the ids of the rows
  they created (`itemId`, `parentId`) rather than by time, so they do not depend on the
  clock or on entries from earlier runs, and they can run repeatedly against a shared
  environment.

  ## Personas and fixtures

  Seed data comes from `testdata/enduser-activitylog/<env>.js`, which only maps personas
  from the shared fixtures to the roles below:

  - connections: a delegator and a receiver, both private persons. No other suite pairs
    them, so the scenario owns the connection between the two.
  - request: the requester and organization of the DraftAndCounts scenarios, and the ttd
    service owner, which creates and withdraws the draft.
  - maskinporten: a consumer organization acting through its daglig leder and a supplier
    organization acting through its innehaver. No other suite pairs them.
  - outsider: a private person with no relation to any of the parties.

  Every step mints its own token through `helpers.js` (`loginAs(persona, scopes?)` and
  `loginAsServiceOwner()`). `helpers.js` also holds the activity type catalog ids, which
  are fixed in code and the same in every environment.

  ## Behaviour worth knowing

  - Authorization runs before validation. A query without `direction`, or with
    `direction=via`, is refused with 403 rather than 400, because the scope policies pick
    the required scope from the direction.
  - `links.next` is built from the host the service sees, which behind the platform
    gateway is an internal host name. The pagination steps check only the path and query
    of the link, and ask for the next page with `pageNo` directly.
  - A bearer token with three dot-separated parts that is not a JWT (`abc.def.ghi`)
    returns 500 from every Access Management endpoint. Scenario 05 asserts 401 only for a
    token that is not a JWT at all.

  ## Environments

  Verified on AT22. The TT02 seed file maps the same personas from the TT02 versions of
  the shared fixtures, but is not verified yet: the activity log routes returned 404 on
  TT02 when it was added (October 2026). The routes sit behind the feature
  flags `AccessManagement.Enduser.ConnectionsActivityLogApi`,
  `AccessManagement.Enduser.RequestActivityLogApi` and
  `AccessManagement.Enduser.MaskinportenActivityLogApi`; with a flag off, that area
  returns 404. Until the flags are on in TT02, this folder's pre-request script skips
  every request when the environment is TT02, because the Azure DevOps post-deployment
  run executes the whole `test` folder there.
}
